@HostAddress = http://localhost:5125

### 0. Service info — lists the available endpoints
GET {{HostAddress}}/
Accept: application/json

### 1. List products (helper) — use this to find product ids for creating an invoice
#    Each product carries its brand and stock.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/products
Accept: application/json

### 1b. Brands — GENERATED extension, brand.Map<BrandDto>(mapper) creates a new DTO.
#     Three things to look at in the response. "isoCode": the entity spells it ISOCode and the
#     DTO spells it IsoCode, so this is case-insensitive matching working end to end, through
#     the database. "foundedYear": quoted, because the entity holds it as an int and the DTO
#     as a string — ShiftMapper converted it, invariantly, on its own. "tags": the entity
#     holds a List<string>, the DTO an IReadOnlyList<string>, and the DTO gets its OWN copy
#     rather than a read-only window onto the list EF is still tracking.
#
#     And "externalIds", which is the point of SM0010: the entity holds them as long and the
#     DTO declares them int, so any id past int.MaxValue maps to a different number — no
#     exception, nothing in the code to say so. That is why SM0010 is a build WARNING.
#     The seeded ids sit inside int range, because the nested projection (3c) reaches this
#     same BrandDto four levels down and SQL REFUSES the overflow rather than wrapping it:
#     "Arithmetic overflow error converting expression to data type int". Worth knowing —
#     the conversion that quietly returns the wrong number in C# is a hard error in SQL.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/brands
Accept: application/json

### 1b2. One brand — the other overload, brand.Map(existingDto, mapper) copies onto an
#     object that already exists. "updatedInPlace" proves it is the same instance, and
#     "mapperDependency" proves AppMapper really is a DI service with a dependency.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/brands/1
Accept: application/json

### 1c. Stocks — same generated maps, but called DIRECTLY on the mapper instance
#     (mapper.Map<StockDto>(stock)) instead of through the extension method.
#     Look at "bayNumbers": the entity holds List<int>, the DTO IReadOnlyList<string>, so both
#     the collection shape and the element type were converted — and the POST below reads them
#     back the other way.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/stocks
Accept: application/json

### 1c2. One stock — the instance form of the update overload, mapper.Map(stock, existing)
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/stocks/1
Accept: application/json

### 1c3. Create a stock — the REVERSE map, StockDto -> Stock. Every request above goes
#     entity -> DTO; this one only compiles because AppMapper chains .ReverseMap().
#     The id below is ignored — the endpoint resets it so the database assigns the real one.
#     It is also the direction that has to READ the id back out of text: Stock.Id is an int
#     and StockDto.Id is a string, so the reverse map parses it. Leaving "id" out sends
#     empty text, which reads back as 0; send "id": "not-a-number" to watch it refuse.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/stocks
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Duhok Distribution Centre",
  "city": "Duhok",
  "code": "DHK-DC",
  "bayNumbers": ["1", "2", "3"]
}

### 1c4. Read back what 1c3 created (id 5 on a fresh database) — both directions of the
#     round trip come from that one CreateMap line.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/stocks/5
Accept: application/json

### 1c5. Validation — a stock with no name returns 400 before any mapping happens
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/stocks
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "",
  "city": "Nowhere",
  "code": "NON-E"
}

### 2. List all invoices — full graph: invoice -> lines -> product -> brand + stock
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices
Accept: application/json

### 3. Get a single invoice by id — full graph
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/1
Accept: application/json

### 3b. IGNORE + MAPFROM, mapped in memory — mapper.Map<InvoiceDto>(invoice)
#     Invoice -> InvoiceDto does not map by name alone, and AppMapper settles both problems
#     on one chain:
#
#         CreateMap<Invoice, InvoiceDto>()
#             .Ignore(d => d.Lines)
#             .MapFrom(d => d.Total,  s => s.Lines.Sum(l => l.Quantity * l.UnitPrice))
#             .MapFrom(d => d.Number, s => _numbering.Prefix + s.Number);
#
#     In the response:
#
#       "total"   a number the entity never stores — MapFrom worked it out from the lines.
#       "number"  carries the "IQ/" prefix, which came from IInvoiceNumbering. That service
#                 was injected into AppMapper's CONSTRUCTOR and used inside the expression;
#                 nothing else was needed to make DI work in a custom mapping.
#       "lines"   the whole graph, four levels deep, and NOTHING in the CreateMap chain
#                 mentions it. A nested object maps as soon as a CreateMap exists for its
#                 pair — Invoice -> InvoiceDto pulls in InvoiceLine -> InvoiceLineDto, which
#                 pulls in Product -> ProductDto, which pulls in Brand and Stock.
#
#     Look inside one line and you can see every earlier feature still working, four levels
#     down: "lineTotal" is that map's OWN MapFrom, "foundedYear" is a string over the
#     entity's int, and "bayNumbers" is IReadOnlyList<string> over a List<int>.
#
#     The Lines property is an IReadOnlyList<InvoiceLineDto> over a List<InvoiceLine>, so the
#     collection SHAPE is converted too — the same helpers a List<int> goes through.
#
#     Every Include in this endpoint is load-bearing: an in-memory map can only copy what was
#     fetched, so dropping the ThenInclude for Brand makes the DTO's brand arrive null.
#     Request 3c needs no Includes at all.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/1/mapped
Accept: application/json

### 3c. THE SAME MAP AS A DATABASE QUERY — db.Invoices.ProjectTo<InvoiceDto>(mapper)
#     Same CreateMap line as 3b, no second declaration. The difference is where the work
#     happens: 3b loads whole invoices and maps them in C#; this hands the map itself to EF,
#     which turns it into the SELECT list.
#
#     So the database reads only the columns InvoiceDto uses, adds the lines up itself, and
#     works out every join from the map — there is not one Include in the endpoint, and the
#     nested graph still arrives complete. Compare the JSON with 3b: identical, four levels
#     down, from a declaration that never mentions Product, Brand or Stock.
#
#     ProjectTo returns an IQueryable, so the endpoint's OrderByDescending and Take(20) are
#     part of that same one query rather than filtering in memory afterwards.
#
#     Invoice 1 in this response reads "total": 1596.0000 — the same number 3b produced, only
#     at the database column's scale instead of the C# decimal's.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/projected
Accept: application/json

### 3d. THE SQL ITSELF — what EF made of the map. This is the request to read carefully.
#
#         DECLARE @p int = 20;
#         DECLARE @_numbering_Prefix nvarchar(4000) = N'IQ/';
#
#         SELECT TOP(@p) [i].[Id], [i].[CustomerName], [i].[CustomerEmail], [i].[IssuedAt], (
#             SELECT COALESCE(SUM(CAST([i0].[Quantity] AS decimal(18,2)) * [i0].[UnitPrice]), 0.0)
#             FROM [InvoiceLines] AS [i0]
#             WHERE [i].[Id] = [i0].[InvoiceId]) AS [Total],
#             @_numbering_Prefix + [i].[Number] AS [Number]
#         FROM [Invoices] AS [i]
#         ORDER BY [i].[IssuedAt] DESC
#
#     The nesting turns this into a much bigger query, and every part of it is worth a look:
#
#       * LEFT JOIN to a subquery over [InvoiceLines], then INNER JOIN [Products], [Brands]
#         and [Stocks]. FOUR LEVELS OF JOINS that no endpoint asked for — EF worked them out
#         from the map, because the whole nested graph reached it as ONE expression.
#       * CAST([i1].[Quantity] AS decimal(18,2)) * [i1].[UnitPrice] — that is InvoiceLineDto's
#         own MapFrom for LineTotal, composed in from the NESTED map. A customization two
#         levels down still translates.
#       * CONVERT(varchar(11), [b].[FoundedYear]) — the int-to-string conversion, at level 4.
#       * OUTER APPLY OPENJSON(...) for Tags, ExternalIds and BayNumbers, the primitive
#         collections, also at level 4.
#       * The MapFrom for Total is still its own CORRELATED SUBQUERY, and
#         @_numbering_Prefix is still the injected service arriving as a parameter named
#         after your field.
#
#     One round trip for all of it.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/projected?sql=true
Accept: text/plain

### 3e. WHERE THE LIMIT ACTUALLY IS — this one needs an edit to see, and it is worth doing.
#     In AppMapper.cs swap the last MapFrom for the OTHER member of the same service:
#
#         .MapFrom(d => d.Number, s => _numbering.Format(s.Number, s.IssuedAt))
#
#     Format takes the ROW's data, so no database can run it. Re-run 3c and it still works:
#     EF is allowed to evaluate a top-level projection on the client, so it selects the
#     columns Format needs and runs your C# per row. One query, only the needed columns —
#     run 3d to watch [i].[Number] and [i].[IssuedAt] come back raw instead of concatenated.
#
#     The break comes one step further out. Add a filter on that property in the endpoint:
#
#         .ProjectTo<InvoiceDto>(mapper).Where(i => i.Number.Contains("2026"))
#
#     and this request returns 500:
#
#         InvalidOperationException: The LINQ expression could not be translated.
#         Translation of method 'IInvoiceNumbering.Format' failed.
#
#     A Where decides which rows the database returns, so it cannot wait until they have
#     arrived. With Prefix there is no such limit, because that value really is in the SQL.
#
#     ShiftMapper predicts none of this — it generates the projection and lets EF answer, so
#     you get what today's EF can do rather than what ShiftMapper assumed when it was written.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/projected
Accept: application/json

### 3f. THE TWO RULES NESTING ADDS — both are edits rather than requests, both worth doing.
#
#     (a) A NESTED OBJECT WITH NO MAP IS A BUILD ERROR.
#
#     Comment out CreateMap<Product, ProductDto>() in AppMapper.cs and the build STOPS:
#
#         error SM0011: 'InvoiceLineDto.Product' needs a map from 'Product' to 'ProductDto'.
#                       Add CreateMap<Product, ProductDto>() to this mapper, or
#                       .Ignore(d => d.Product) to leave it unmapped on purpose
#
#     This is the only ERROR ShiftMapper reports. Every other message describes a property
#     left unmapped, which may well be on purpose; this one describes a property that cannot
#     be mapped YET, where a null in the response would be indistinguishable from a null in
#     the database. Both ways out are one line, and both leave the decision written down.
#
#     (b) MaxDepth STOPS THE GRAPH, and defaults to 10.
#
#     Set it on this map:
#
#         CreateMap<Invoice, InvoiceDto>(o => o.MaxDepth = 2)
#
#     Re-run 3c and "lines" comes back empty, because the graph under it needs 4 levels:
#
#         info SM0012: 'InvoiceDto.Lines' needs 4 levels and this map allows 2, so it is
#                      left unmapped. Raise MaxDepth, or .Ignore(d => d.Lines) to say so
#                      deliberately
#
#     Informational rather than a warning, because stopping is what a limit is for — use
#     `dotnet build -v d` to see it. It sets the same way as case-sensitivity does: on one
#     CreateMap, on a ReverseMap, or for the whole mapper through ConfigureDefaults.
#
#     Note the property is dropped ENTIRELY rather than filled part of the way. In memory a
#     nested object is mapped by CALLING the mapper's own Map method, which cannot be asked
#     to stop halfway — so filling it in memory while cutting it short in SQL would make one
#     declaration mean two different things.
#
#     MaxDepth is also what makes DTOs that point back at each other terminate: a BrandDto
#     holding ProductDtos, each holding a BrandDto, has a graph no limit can finish, so the
#     loop is cut and SM0012 says where. .Ignore on one side records which is the view and
#     which is the back-reference.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/projected
Accept: application/json

### 4. Create an invoice
#    Only product id + quantity are sent; the server snapshots the current price.
#    The response is the created invoice with the full related graph.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customerName": "Layla Ibrahim",
  "customerEmail": "layla.ibrahim@example.com",
  "lines": [
    { "productId": 2, "quantity": 1 },
    { "productId": 10, "quantity": 2 },
    { "productId": 23, "quantity": 4 }
  ]
}

### 5. Get the invoice that was just created (id 4 on a fresh database)
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/4
Accept: application/json

### 6. Not found — asking for an invoice that does not exist returns 404
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/9999
Accept: application/json

### 7. Validation — empty lines returns 400
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customerName": "No Lines",
  "customerEmail": "no.lines@example.com",
  "lines": []
}

### 8. Validation — unknown product id returns 400
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customerName": "Bad Product",
  "customerEmail": "bad.product@example.com",
  "lines": [
    { "productId": 99999, "quantity": 1 }
  ]
}
